INSERT INTO feature VALUES('support-by-weakening','kernel','Support by weakening','kernel-admission','implemented','notable',NULL,'The kernel checks each declaration against only the entries its content reaches: the check starts at the positions its content names, an entry it reads adds the positions that entry names, and a sort adds its types of words. A read outside them refuses the declaration `OutsideSupport` at that position, and no check walks the environment. A check reads only the entries ranked below the declaration it admits: an entry ranked at or above it reads as no entry, and so do the types of words and the role holders ranked there. The reach of an edit is the edited entries and each entry whose support holds one of them, read backward through a reverse index of the direct references: a type of words reaches through its sort only the entries after it. The walk reads the rows of the reach alone. A readmission of an edit shares with its record the leading run that the edit leaves, as a resumed readmission does, and reads nothing in it. Past the run, it retires the reach, moves each live entry to the rank of its place in the edited source above the run, keeps each other entry in place on its own key, and offers the reach alone. A write to one kept entry copies at most 64 slots, those of the chunk that holds it. A kept entry answers on its held key by lookup while nothing ranked at or below it changes since the kernel checked that key. A retired entry keeps its position and frees its declaration, its key and its rows. The kernel keys each member of a sealed module when its seal closes, and an edit that touches a seal readmits whole. The witnesses of a formation keep just below their first reader, named by the digest of their formation''s content. An edit of that reader retires them and edits each reader of the formation, so the first reader that crosses again forms it under the same name. The name reads each constant that the kernel names by its position by that constant''s content key, so a formation over a datatype, an atom or an axiom keeps its witnesses too, under one name across kernel builds. A walk under a policy that rechecks keys each admission as a walk under keys does, and names each plain definition that its formations read again over those keys, in one walk. It walks again with keys only when such a definition reads a constant that shares its key with another. A formation that reads a constant with no content key keeps no witness past the run.','kernel-core kernel-core-base kernel-term core-checker-bridge','crates/kernel-core-base/src/env.rs crates/kernel-core-base/src/encoding.rs crates/kernel-core/src/environment.rs crates/kernel-core/src/dependents.rs crates/kernel-term/src/arena.rs crates/kernel-term/src/chunked.rs crates/core-checker-bridge/src/bridge/keep.rs crates/core-checker-bridge/src/bridge/tests.rs',NULL,NULL);
